Two follow-ups to the blocking flip, both surfaced by its own CI run.

INSTRUMENT DRIFT WAS ALREADY LIVE. The dev extras in hypergumbo-core and
hypergumbo-tracker declared `mypy~=2.1`, which admits 2.2 and 2.3. A fresh
CI install resolved mypy 2.3.0 while the committed baseline was measured
locally at 2.1.0 — so the instrument already differed between a developer
machine and a clean install, silently, with the ratchet comparing counts
across the two and attributing the difference to code. The mypy job's own
explicit pin was the only thing keeping that job honest. Both dev extras
are now pinned to ~=2.1.0 so one series is used everywhere, and adopting a
newer mypy becomes a deliberate change that must carry its own re-measured
baseline — which is exactly what the new mismatch check exists to force.

This is the risk WI-rabum was holding the flip for, found to be not
hypothetical but already in effect.

